<p>Section 01 • Foundations</p>
<h2>What is a Modular Kitchen — and Why Does it Matter?</h2>
<p>A <strong>modular kitchen</strong> is a pre-fabricated, factory-made kitchen system built from standardized cabinet modules — upper units, lower units, tall units, and corner units — that are assembled on-site. Unlike a site-built (carpenter-made) kitchen where every piece is cut and fitted in your home, modular kitchens are precision-manufactured in a controlled environment, then installed like a jigsaw puzzle.</p>
<p>The word "modular" is key: each cabinet is an independent, self-contained box (called a <em>carcass</em>) that can be mixed, matched, and reconfigured. This means your kitchen can be planned down to the millimetre, scaled up or down, and even partially dismantled and moved.</p>
<p>40%</p>
<p>of kitchen time is spent searching for things</p>
<p>3×</p>
<p>more usable space vs an unplanned kitchen</p>
<p>7–10 yrs</p>
<p>typical lifespan of a well-built modular kitchen</p>
<h3>Modular vs Carpenter-Made Kitchen</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Modular Kitchen</th><th>Carpenter-Made</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Manufacturing</td><td>Factory precision, CNC-cut</td><td>On-site, hand-cut</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Installation Time</td><td>3–7 days</td><td>15–25 days</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dust &amp; Mess On-site</td><td>Minimal</td><td>High</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Uniform finish every time</td><td>Varies by craftsman</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Customisation</td><td>Within module sizes (600mm grid)</td><td>Any size, any shape</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Odd-Shaped Rooms</td><td>Needs filler strips</td><td>Better for irregular spaces</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dismantling &amp; Relocation</td><td>Possible</td><td>Not possible</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Long-term Durability</td><td>Higher (factory QC)</td><td>Depends on carpenter skill</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">When to choose a carpenter-made kitchen instead</span>💡</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>If your kitchen is very small (under 50 sq ft), has highly irregular walls, pipework obstacles, or a very tight budget — a skilled local carpenter may actually deliver better results than a modular system forced into an awkward space.</div></div>
<p>Section 02 • Planning</p>
<h2>Kitchen Layout Types — Which One is Right for Your Space?</h2>
<p>The layout you choose determines everything downstream: workflow efficiency, storage capacity, ventilation placement, and how sociable your kitchen feels. There is no universally "best" layout — the right one depends entirely on your room shape, family size, and cooking habits.</p>
<h4>Straight / Single Wall</h4>
<p>All cabinets along one wall. Efficient in a 6–8 ft run. Works well as an open kitchen adjacent to dining.</p>
<h4>L-Shape</h4>
<p>Two adjacent walls. The most common layout for Indian 2BHKs. Great workflow with 8–14 running ft total.</p>
<h4>U-Shape</h4>
<p>Three walls. Maximum counter space and storage. Ideal for serious cooks but requires min. 8×8 ft room.</p>
<h4>Parallel / Galley</h4>
<p>Two facing walls. Extremely efficient for narrow kitchens but feels cramped with more than one cook.</p>
<h4>Island / Peninsula</h4>
<p>A detached counter in the centre. Requires min. 10×12 ft. Adds prep space and doubles as social area.</p>
<h4>G-Shape</h4>
<p>U-shape with a partial 4th wall or peninsula. Maximum storage and workflow but needs a large room.</p>
<h3>How to Choose Your Layout: 4 Key Questions</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Q1 <strong>What is the minimum aisle width?</strong> Single cook needs 900mm aisle minimum; two cooks together need 1200mm. Measure your room before picking a layout.</li>
  <li>Q2 <strong>Where is the window and exhaust point?</strong> Your hob must be near a window or an external wall for the chimney duct to exit. This often dictates the layout more than aesthetics.</li>
  <li>Q3 <strong>Where are the plumbing inlets/drain points?</strong> Relocating drain points is expensive and creates future leak risks. Design your sink location around the existing drain stub-out wherever possible.</li>
  <li>Q4 <strong>How many people cook simultaneously?</strong> Joint families or couples who cook together need fundamentally different layouts than a single-cook household. More cooks = wider aisles + more prep counter.</li>
</ol>
<p>Section 03 • Ergonomics</p>
<h2>The Kitchen Work Triangle — Still the Most Important Rule</h2>
<p>The <strong>kitchen work triangle</strong> connects the three primary work zones: the <em>sink</em>, the <em>hob</em>, and the <em>refrigerator</em>. This concept, developed by the University of Illinois in the 1940s, remains the single most validated ergonomic principle in kitchen design.</p>
<h4>The Rules of the Triangle</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Each side: 1.2–2.1 m</strong> — no leg should be too short (cramped) or too long (exhausting)</li>
  <li><strong>Total perimeter: 4–7.9 m</strong> — ideal range for flow efficiency</li>
  <li><strong>No walkways through the triangle</strong> — traffic cuts kill kitchen efficiency</li>
  <li><strong>Never put hob adjacent to fridge</strong> — heat and compressor don't mix</li>
  <li><strong>Prep zone is at the sink</strong> — most chopping and washing happens here</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The triangle breaks down for island kitchens</span>⚠️</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>Modern kitchen planning has evolved toward a <strong>zone-based approach</strong> for larger kitchens: a Prep Zone, a Cooking Zone, a Cleaning Zone, and a Storage Zone. This works better when you have an island or two cooks. The triangle is still the best starting point for small kitchens under 100 sq ft.</div></div>
<p>Section 04 • Dimensions</p>
<h2>Critical Measurements Every Kitchen Needs to Get Right</h2>
<p>Incorrect dimensions are the single biggest source of regret in kitchen design. These aren't just numbers — they affect how your back feels after cooking for two hours, whether you bump your head on upper cabinets, and whether two people can actually work in the kitchen together.</p>
