<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The custom counter height rule</span>📏</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>The standard 850mm counter height was designed for an average 170cm person. If your primary cook is significantly shorter or taller, ask your designer to adjust. For a 155cm person, the ideal counter is around 820mm. For a 185cm person, 900mm. This single change can eliminate back pain during long cooking sessions.</div></div>
<p>Section 05 • Materials</p>
<h2>Cabinet Carcass Materials — The Foundation of Your Kitchen</h2>
<p>The <strong>carcass</strong> (also called the cabinet box or carcase) is the structural frame of every cabinet. Once your kitchen is installed, you'll never see it again — but it determines how long your kitchen lasts, how it handles moisture, and whether it warps, delaminates, or collapses in 5 years. This is where you should never compromise.</p>
<h4>BWP Plywood (IS:710 / 710-grade)</h4>
<p>Boiling Water Proof ply. The gold standard for kitchen carcasses. Made with phenol-formaldehyde resin — won't delaminate even with prolonged moisture exposure.</p>
<p>Durability — 95/100</p>
<p>Moisture resistance — 90/100</p>
<p>Cost efficiency — 45/100 (expensive)</p>
<h4>BWR Plywood (IS:303)</h4>
<p>Boiling Water Resistant. Very good for dry-area cabinets (wardrobes, TV units). Acceptable for kitchens in low-humidity zones but avoid under-sink areas.</p>
<p>Durability — 80/100</p>
<p>Moisture resistance — 70/100</p>
<p>Cost efficiency — 65/100</p>
<h4>HDHMR (High Density High Moisture Resistant)</h4>
<p>Factory-treated board — denser than standard MDF, with added moisture resistance. Excellent for shutters and back panels; increasingly used for carcasses in premium kitchens.</p>
<p>Durability — 78/100</p>
<p>Moisture resistance — 80/100</p>
<p>Cost efficiency — 70/100</p>
<h4>Marine Ply</h4>
<p>Strongest available — used in boat-building. Overkill for most kitchens but genuinely the best choice for under-sink cabinets in wet zones or coastal areas.</p>
<p>Durability — 98/100</p>
<p>Moisture resistance — 98/100</p>
<p>Cost efficiency — 20/100 (very expensive)</p>
<h4>MDF (Medium Density Fibreboard)</h4>
<p>Good for shutters and painted surfaces. Never use as carcass in a kitchen — it absorbs moisture, swells at edges, and loses screw-holding strength after the first year.</p>
<p>Durability — 60/100 (shutter use only)</p>
<p>Moisture resistance — 25/100</p>
<p>Cost efficiency — 80/100</p>
<h4>Particle Board / Pre-Lam</h4>
<p>Made from wood chips glued together. Cheap and readily available. Should not be used in any kitchen environment — swells noticeably in 18–24 months, delaminate, and loses structural integrity. A false economy.</p>
<p>Durability — 35/100</p>
<p>Moisture resistance — 10/100</p>
<p>Cost efficiency — 90/100 (cheap, not good)</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The particle board trap — how to spot it</span>🚫</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>Contractors often quote using "pre-laminated board" without specifying it's particle board. Always ask: <em>"What IS standard is this carcass material? What is the density in kg/m³?"</em> BWP plywood is 700–800 kg/m³. Particle board is 650–750 kg/m³ but has drastically lower moisture resistance. If they can't answer, walk away.</div></div>
<p>Section 06 • Finishes</p>
<h2>Shutter Finishes — The Face of Your Kitchen</h2>
<p>Shutters are what you see and touch every day. The finish determines the kitchen's aesthetic, how easy it is to clean, how scratch-resistant it is, and whether it ages gracefully or poorly. Here's every finish type worth knowing:</p>
