<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Plan your duct route before the kitchen is designed</span>⚠️</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>The chimney duct must exit through an external wall, terrace, or common duct shaft. In many apartments, the duct route is 2–4 metres long, running through a false ceiling. This route must be planned at the design stage — trying to add it after installation means opening up ceilings and walls. Your designer should mark the duct path in the kitchen drawing.</div></div>
<p>Section 11 • Lighting</p>
<h2>Kitchen Lighting — Layer by Layer</h2>
<p>Kitchen lighting is universally under-planned. Most kitchens end up with a single overhead light that creates shadows exactly where you're chopping vegetables. Good kitchen lighting has at least three layers, each serving a different purpose.</p>
<p>☀️</p>
<h4>Ambient / General</h4>
<p>Overhead lighting for the whole room. Recessed downlights or a ceiling light. Aim for 300–500 lux uniformly. This is your base layer.</p>
<p>🔦</p>
<h4>Task Lighting</h4>
<p>Directly illuminates the work surface. Under-cabinet LED strips are the standard. Position them at the front edge of upper cabinets to eliminate counter shadows.</p>
<p>✨</p>
<h4>Accent / Mood</h4>
<p>Inside glass-front cabinets, above loft units, toe-kick lighting. Creates depth and warmth. Low impact on function but high impact on the kitchen's perceived quality.</p>
<h3>Colour Temperature — The Kitchen Choice</h3>
