<p>Section 12 • Appliances</p>
<h2>Appliance Integration — Plan These Before You Finalise Cabinets</h2>
<p>This is where most kitchen planning fails: appliances are selected after the kitchen is designed, creating a cascade of "I didn't leave space for the oven" and "the refrigerator door hits the cabinet door" problems. The golden rule: <strong>decide your appliance list and get exact specifications before a single cabinet is drawn.</strong></p>
<h3>Appliances That Affect Cabinet Design Directly</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Built-in Oven</strong> Requires a tall unit cavity at 595mm width × 595mm height × 550mm depth. Positioned at ergonomic height (top of oven at eye level ≈ 1400mm from floor). Needs a 15A dedicated electrical point inside the cavity. Decide gas vs electric before design.</li>
  <li><strong>Microwave</strong> Standard size: 560mm wide × 380mm tall × 450mm deep. If built-in, needs a dedicated shelf in the tall unit at a comfortable height (shoulder to waist level). A microwave at counter level is the most ergonomically problematic placement — avoid it.</li>
  <li><strong>Dishwasher</strong> Standard: 600mm wide × 820mm tall × 550mm deep. Requires hot water inlet + drain connection. Goes next to the sink, never separated from it. Leave the cabinet slot before installation — a dishwasher added later requires major kitchen surgery.</li>
  <li><strong>Refrigerator</strong> Critical: measure your fridge and add 25mm clearance on each side and 50mm at the back for air circulation. A fridge pushed against a wall or cabinet with zero clearance overheats the compressor and fails early. Never place directly adjacent to the hob — the heat degrades efficiency.</li>
  <li><strong>Hob (Gas vs Induction vs Hybrid)</strong> Standard gas hob: 600–700mm wide × 50–80mm thick. Induction: much thinner (10–15mm countertop cutout). Hybrid (2 gas + 2 induction): available in 60cm and 90cm widths. Choose hob size first — it anchors the counter layout and chimney size.</li>
  <li><strong>Water Purifier / RO Unit</strong> Under-counter RO: requires 400mm clearance under the sink and a dedicated 5A socket inside the cabinet. Over-counter unit: takes up approximately 300mm of valuable counter space. Decide placement at design stage — the drain tube and water feed must be routed into the cabinet.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Electrical points — plan for more than you think you need</span>🔌</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>A typical Indian kitchen needs: chimney, refrigerator, microwave, oven, mixer-grinder, toaster, electric kettle, RO unit, dishwasher, and often an induction cooktop backup. That's <strong>10+ points</strong> just for appliances. Add under-cabinet lighting, and a counter USB port. Plan at least 12–15 electrical points in the kitchen at the drawing stage — adding them after cabinets are installed means opening walls.</div></div>
<p>Section 13 • What to Avoid</p>
<h2>12 Modular Kitchen Mistakes That Are Completely Avoidable</h2>
<ul>
  <li>1<h4>Placing the hob directly under the window</h4>
<p>Curtains catch fire, wind disrupts the flame, and direct light creates glare on the countertop. Leave at least 200mm between a gas hob and any window or curtain. The chimney should also not be installed in front of a window — it blocks light and makes cleaning very difficult.</p></li>
  <li>2<h4>Not waterproofing behind the sink cabinet</h4>
<p>Water always finds its way behind the sink area — from leaks, condensation, and dishwashing. Without waterproofing the cabinet interior and the wall behind it, you'll see mould and ply delamination within 3–4 years. This is a 15-minute fix during installation that is almost never done unless you insist.</p></li>
  <li>3<h4>Choosing countertop colour and shutter finish at the showroom</h4>
<p>Showroom lighting is deliberately flattering and almost universally warmer than natural kitchen light. Always bring countertop and shutter samples home and view them in your actual kitchen space — morning light, afternoon light, and under your planned kitchen lighting.</p></li>
  <li>4<h4>Installing all upper cabinets at the same height</h4>
<p>When the loft unit and the wall cabinets are at the same height with no visual break, the kitchen looks heavy and hospital-like. Vary heights slightly — bring some upper cabinets down to 450mm clearance and leave others at full height near the ceiling for visual rhythm.</p></li>
  <li>5<h4>Skimping on counter space to fit more cabinets</h4>
<p>An Indian cook needs at least 900mm of continuous, uninterrupted counter space for chopping, rolling, and prep. Every additional 300mm of counter makes cooking measurably easier. Prioritise counter space over extra cabinets — you can add a pantry unit or rolling cart for storage later.</p></li>
  <li>6<h4>Buying the chimney after the kitchen is installed</h4>
<p>The chimney, its duct, and its ceiling hole must be planned before the false ceiling is done. A chimney decided after installation often ends up with a non-optimal duct route, a visible surface-run duct, or a hole cut in the wrong place.</p></li>
  <li>7<h4>Using dark shutter colours in a small kitchen</h4>
<p>Dark cabinets in a kitchen under 70 sq ft make the space feel claustrophobic and make grease deposits invisible until they're severe. If you love dark finishes, use them on lower cabinets only, with lighter uppers — this grounds the space without shrinking it.</p></li>
  <li>8<h4>Ignoring the backsplash-countertop joint</h4>
<p>The junction between the countertop and the backsplash tiles is the most common source of water ingress into the kitchen wall. This joint must be filled with a food-safe silicone sealant, not tile grout. Grout cracks within 6 months at this joint; silicone remains flexible and watertight.</p></li>
  <li>9<h4>Not planning for waste segregation</h4>
<p>India's waste segregation requirements (wet vs dry waste) make a built-in pull-out waste bin with two compartments effectively essential in a new kitchen. Retrofitting a drawer for this purpose after installation is difficult. Plan a 300–400mm cabinet near the prep zone or sink for a dual-bin pull-out.</p></li>
  <li>10<h4>Overlooking the kitchen's relationship to the dining space</h4>
<p>An open kitchen or a kitchen with a pass-through window must be designed in relation to the dining area — traffic flow, visual sight lines, and smell management all matter. A parallel kitchen with a window into the dining space can eliminate unnecessary trips and improve family communication during meals.</p></li>
  <li>11<h4>Accepting the first 3D render as final</h4>
<p>Most kitchen designers generate a 3D visualisation in 2–3 days. This render often uses standard product images and doesn't accurately show your specific material selections in your room's actual light. Request at least 2 iterations and ask to see the design with and without upper cabinets on one wall to evaluate both options.</p></li>
  <li>12<h4>No single-item shutdown valve for the kitchen water supply</h4>
<p>Your kitchen has multiple water connections: sink tap, dishwasher, RO unit, potentially a water purifier tap. Every one of these should branch off a single shutoff valve that's accessible without moving anything. This prevents minor plumbing repairs from requiring complete counter access or a plumber visit every time.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Section 14 • Checklist</p>
<h2>The Pre-Design Kitchen Planning Checklist</h2>
<p>Before your first meeting with a designer or contractor, gather answers to these questions. They'll save you 2–3 revision cycles and prevent the most common mid-project surprises.</p>
<h3>Site &amp; Space</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Exact kitchen dimensions</strong> Measure wall-to-wall at floor, mid-wall, and ceiling level. Walls are rarely perfectly square.</li>
  <li><strong>Location of all doors and windows</strong> Including width, height, and distance from adjacent walls.</li>
  <li><strong>Drain point and water inlet location</strong> Mark exact positions — these anchor the sink and dishwasher location.</li>
  <li><strong>Existing electrical points</strong> Count and mark them; identify if any need to move.</li>
  <li><strong>Gas pipeline inlet location</strong> The hob must be reachable from this point without running a pipe across the kitchen.</li>
  <li><strong>External wall for chimney duct</strong> Confirm which wall is external and where a 150mm hole can be drilled.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Family &amp; Lifestyle</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Who are the primary cooks?</strong> Height, cooking style (Indian heavy cooking vs light continental), physical limitations.</li>
  <li><strong>How often do you cook vs order in?</strong> A daily-cooking family needs radically more durable materials than a household that cooks twice a week.</li>
  <li><strong>How many people use the kitchen simultaneously?</strong> Determines aisle width requirements.</li>
  <li><strong>Do you have a domestic helper who uses the kitchen?</strong> Affects storage organization and counter height considerations.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Appliance List</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>All appliances with exact model dimensions</strong> Don't give this list to the designer as "refrigerator, microwave" — give brand and model number.</li>
  <li><strong>Built-in vs freestanding oven decision</strong> This changes the tall unit design completely.</li>
  <li><strong>Gas hob vs induction vs hybrid</strong> Affects chimney clearance, electrical requirements, and countertop cutout size.</li>
  <li><strong>Dishwasher — yes or no?</strong> If yes, mark the slot now. If maybe later, leave a 600mm blind panel that can be replaced.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Design Preferences</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>5 reference kitchen photos you love</strong> Pinterest, Houzz, or Instagram saves. Helps the designer understand your aesthetic language faster than any verbal description.</li>
  <li><strong>Colours you definitely don't want</strong> Negative direction is often more useful than positive direction in kitchen design.</li>
  <li><strong>Open shelves yes or no?</strong> Open shelves look great in photos but collect grease and require constant tidiness in a working Indian kitchen.</li>
  <li><strong>Handle style preference</strong> Traditional bar handles, G-profile, or handleless. This shapes the entire aesthetic direction.</li>
</ul>
